---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  description="ThreadSherpa's support boundaries: opted-in channels, explicit knowledge saves, bounded lookup tools, and what moderators remain responsible for."
>
  <header class="page-hero">
    <div class="wrap page-hero__inner">
      <p class="kicker">Security boundaries</p>
      <h1>Helpful is not the same as trusted.</h1>
      <p class="page-hero__lede">
        ThreadSherpa narrows what can become memory, what can be looked up, and who can change it.
        These boundaries are part of the product, not a promise that generated answers are always
        correct.
      </p>
      <div class="page-hero__actions">
        <a class="btn btn--primary" href="/setup/">Read the setup guide</a>
        <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/#how-it-works">How it works</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <div class="page-body">
    <div class="wrap guide-layout">
      <aside class="guide-toc" aria-label="On this page">
        <h2>On this page</h2>
        <ol>
          <li><a href="#channels">Opted-in channels</a></li>
          <li><a href="#memory">Explicit memory</a></li>
          <li><a href="#tools">Bounded tools</a></li>
          <li><a href="#people">Human review</a></li>
          <li><a href="#providers">Model providers</a></li>
          <li><a href="#data">Stored data</a></li>
          <li><a href="#limits">What remains fallible</a></li>
        </ol>
      </aside>

      <article class="prose">
        <section id="channels">
          <h2>Only opted-in channels</h2>
          <p>
            ThreadSherpa monitors only support channels a moderator connects. Forum posts use
            their parent channel configuration. <code>/ask</code> is an explicit request and can
            work elsewhere in the server.
          </p>
          <p>
            The application reads new events. It does not backfill historical posts, and it does not
            silently turn an existing archive into a knowledge base.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="memory">
          <h2>Only explicit saves become knowledge</h2>
          <p>
            Ordinary conversation never becomes approved knowledge automatically. A moderator
            saves a specific reply through the native message action or <code>/teach</code>. The
            selected facts become reusable guidance for that server. Saving does not post a reply
            into the old conversation.
          </p>
          <p>
            Saving, correcting, removing, setup, and <code>/queue</code> require Discord
            Administrator or a configured moderator role. An empty moderator-role list does not
            authorize ordinary members. Outcome buttons require the original asker or a moderator.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="tools">
          <h2>Bounded lookup, not an agent with keys</h2>
          <p>
            Models and fetched text cannot execute arbitrary tools. The only lookup tools are
            reviewed knowledge and exact moderator-selected source pages. The fetcher enforces
            domain and SSRF checks, redirect limits, and response limits. Broad web search, GitHub
            publishing, and incident actions are not part of the support path.
          </p>
          <p>
            Citation validation establishes where an answer came from. It does not prove that the
            generated sentence is factually entailed by the source. Read the reply and inspect the
            source before trusting it.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="people">
          <h2>Quiet is a valid answer</h2>
          <p>
            Unsupported questions wait for a moderator instead of receiving a public filler
            message. Need help and the quiet queue do not DM or page staff, and they do not promise
            a response time. Questions are never automatically merged. A clear confirmation from
            the asker can mark the case resolved; uncertain replies stay open.
          </p>
          <p>
            Sensitive account operations, payments, and visible credential assignments take a
            private human-support path before model or web calls. This is a bounded pattern check,
            not a guarantee that every secret or sensitive request is detected.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="providers">
          <h2>Where the question goes</h2>
          <p>
            With Ollama, the synthesis request stays on the machine running the bot. Local
            embeddings also run through Ollama. Discord, MongoDB Atlas, selected documentation
            pages, and optional Sentry still use network services.
          </p>
          <p>
            Paper mode sends synthesis questions and supporting evidence to Paper Instruments.
            Review a provider's data handling before operating with real community data. Provider
            credentials belong in server-side environment variables, never in browser code,
            screenshots, commits, or this website.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="data">
          <h2>What the bot stores</h2>
          <p>
            The bot stores support text, requester IDs, evidence, reviewed answers, versions,
            reviews, and audit records in MongoDB. There is no automatic retention or
            privacy-erasure job. Removing a saved answer stops future replies from using it, but it
            does not erase historical records or messages already posted.
          </p>
          <p>
            Sentry spans intentionally contain operation metadata and numeric usage rather than
            message bodies. Logs and SDK error behavior still require review before production use.
            Keep <code>.env</code> out of version control, screenshots, and submissions.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="limits">
          <h2>What remains fallible</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>Generated prose can be wrong even when the source was retrieved correctly.</li>
            <li>Prompt-injection checks are bounded heuristics, not a complete defense.</li>
            <li>Semantic duplicate detection can miss a near-duplicate answer.</li>
            <li>Permissions depend on the Discord setup; review channel overrides.</li>
            <li>No automatic job erases historical data after a knowledge removal.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            The project is independent and self-hosted. The operator remains responsible for the
            server, the data, the model provider, and the people using it.
          </p>
        </section>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
